# The fork child's state: fork::fork and fork::child_reset (with the after_fork_child steps it calls) read
# these in every child, written or not. fork copies no page-table entry for a page of `.data` or `.bss` the
# parent never touched, so each such page cost the child a read fault (timer::EPOLL alone: LAYOUT-DATA-CLUSTERS-5;
# the rest, LAYOUT-DATA-CLUSTERS-7: release fork child 7, and 8 at the `data 0x3000` pad, against 6 on next).
# On the cluster's pages, which start-up touches, the child reads them without a fault at every pad.
